WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.053
- Hi, everybody.

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.990
I'm just gonna say hello everybody.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:10.740
My name is Jeanne Garbarino

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.530
and I run up science program called

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.210
RockEDU Science Outreach.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.910
And that is in New York City.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.933
And one of the things that we like to do

00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:25.190
as part of RockEDU is hanging
out with kids like you

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:26.620
and talk about science.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:31.310
And I'm so super pumped to introduce you

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:36.310
to scientist Mollie from
another awesome program

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.630
based in New York City called the BioBus.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.180
And the BioBus is special
because it talks about

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.010
microscopes with kids in
schools, just like you.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:51.670
So I'm gonna mute myself

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:55.540
and I'm gonna hand
everything over to Mollie

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.530
because she's our special
scientist for the day.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:00.860
And I would love for
you all to ask questions

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.460
and really pay attention
to what she has going on

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.950
because I think it's
gonna be very exciting.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.900
All right, I'll talk to you guys later.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.134
- [Molllie] Hi everyone.

00:01:12.134 --> 00:01:14.460
So that is correct.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.710
My name is Mollie

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:22.600
and I work onboard
something called the BioBus,

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:28.070
which is a science laboratory
that we've put inside of,

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.390
can you guess where we put it inside?

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.330
Yeah. I see a hand up.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.763
Claire, go ahead.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:39.780
- [Claire] A bus.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:40.613
- [Mollie] Exactly.

00:01:40.613 --> 00:01:41.750
We put it inside of a bus.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.920
Can you imagine, so like,
think about your school bus,

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.060
if you take a school bus to school,

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.840
and take out all the seats

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.380
and instead if you put a whole science lab

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.030
full of tools like this
one right next to me

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:59.480
and things for us to collect samples with

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.080
and that's what we've done.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.970
And some exciting news is that

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:06.750
I'm actually no longer in New York.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:11.070
And in fact, I am trying to
bring us a brand new BioBus

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.100
in Massachusetts.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.870
So this might be something
that in the future

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:20.530
we can even come and visit you with.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:24.479
So I'm from Massachusetts
originally, and I'm here now.

00:02:24.479 --> 00:02:27.230
So not too far away from all of you.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:30.920
And so, what I wanna do today is

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.086
look at some samples that
I collected just outside

00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:34.923
of my apartment.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.610
And it doesn't look like
very much right now.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.143
I'll show you what it looks like.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:45.600
Anything you notice?

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.170
You can throw things in the chat

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:50.700
or if you want, you can raise your hand

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.925
just so that we don't
get too much feedback.

00:02:52.925 --> 00:02:56.533
We can have people one at a
time call out what they see.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.150
What do you notice?

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:00.983
- [Jeanne] Someone in the chat,

00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:04.113
Claire says dirt and Ava asks.

00:03:05.743 --> 00:03:07.243
- Bugs, I love it.

00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:09.380
Those are kind of questions.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:10.470
I wanna try and answer about it.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:11.303
You're right.

00:03:11.303 --> 00:03:13.963
There's some dirt and
there's even I have a larger,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.940
there's something in a plastic cup.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:18.490
Yeah. I like to recycle things.

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:21.310
So this is just from some takeout food,

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.940
and it's become a scientific tool for me.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.810
Did you know that
scientists often use things

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.470
that they, just like the rest of us

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.360
like Tupperware things
they have around the house

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:31.360
as scientific tools?

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:32.530
So this is one.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.650
I went outside today and I collected

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.460
I would call it a leaf litter.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:38.293
I love that.

00:03:38.293 --> 00:03:40.523
"Maybe there's worms in it," Claire says.

00:03:41.887 --> 00:03:42.900
"Moon rocks."

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.870
I wish, that would be cool.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.423
I mean, I don't know
what's in my leaf litter.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.290
It would be difficult for me to identify

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:53.653
if it originally came from the moon.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:57.000
We need a geologist.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.340
- [Jeanne] Mollie, there's
a chat here that says

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:05.340
that somebody is named
McLean named a bug, Bart.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:08.830
- [Mollie] Wow. Okay.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:09.890
I like that.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.083
Has anyone here ever had like,

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:18.100
maybe a thumbs up if you've
tried to keep a bug alive

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:20.970
inside your house before.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.195
Some thumbs up for that.

00:04:22.195 --> 00:04:23.750
And you can also give me a
thumbs down if you haven't.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.190
But just so I can see, who here
has ever tried that before?

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.460
Some yes and nos.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.310
Okay. Who here has ever
tried to keep a plant alive

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:32.763
inside your house before?

00:04:34.636 --> 00:04:37.170
A few more thumbs up with that one.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.490
I see some mes in the chat.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:40.323
That's great.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.340
So when I grew up,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.830
I used to love learning
about living things

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:46.930
by doing exactly that.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.990
I would keep them alive in my house

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.340
which my parents weren't
always super happy about,

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.640
or I'd go outside and look
at them really closely.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.320
And that's a really great way

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.350
for all of you to be a scientist.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:02.240
Just exactly with the things that you have

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.320
with your eyes, and your
ears, and your nose sometimes.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.440
And really, I try not
to use my taste so much

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.900
when I'm observing in science.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:14.693
There's some times that that's appropriate

00:05:14.693 --> 00:05:17.540
but not if I'm looking at bugs.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:21.020
Now, why am I saying all of these things?

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:25.010
Today, I have a special
tool for us to use together.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:26.010
And it's the one, it's behind.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.350
We've already told you what it is called.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:29.183
Throw in the chat.

00:05:29.183 --> 00:05:30.050
Tell me, what is this?

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.660
Do you remember what this tool,

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.703
the name of this scientific
tool right next to me is?

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:39.470
Excellent job.

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:41.330
You got Ava,

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:46.260
got some excellent microscope
answers coming in the chat.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.023
This is a microscope.

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:53.760
And something very special
about it is, I have equipped it

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.130
with a camera on top, which is great

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:00.230
because how would you
all feel if I might look,

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:03.610
this isn't the most fun
tool on earth to use

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:05.130
and I'm just gonna use
it and you can watch me.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.510
Does that sound like a good idea?

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:08.450
I'll just use it.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:09.730
I'll look into it like this.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:12.230
Then you can just all look
at the back of my head.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:15.410
I don't think so.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:19.770
So what we've done instead
is I've made it so that

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:24.770
I can give you all microscope vision.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:30.050
You will be able to see everything
that the microscope sees

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:32.110
and we'll make discoveries together.

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:33.200
I don't know what we'll find.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.040
'Cause like I said, I
just collected the sample

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:39.650
and it could have all
sorts of things in it

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.500
and we're gonna have to discover.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.440
So I'm gonna practice microscope vision

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.220
before I put the sample on.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:46.620
So what I need from all of you,

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.670
there's a little bit of
participation involved.

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:50.460
What I need from all of you

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:51.980
is you're gonna give me a countdown,

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:54.540
three, two, one, and then you point at me

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.740
and I'm gonna get the
microscope initialized

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:01.210
and ready to show onto the screening.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:04.453
And when I do that,

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:10.540
right now, nothing on the microscope

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.060
and I'm gonna put a mystery object

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:14.980
and we're gonna look at that
with our microscope vision.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:15.813
Okay?

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.140
I will give you one hint.

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:22.410
The mystery object is something
I imagine you all have

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:24.290
very nearby right now.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:26.120
So let's do a countdown.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:27.070
Ready?

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:32.070
Can you give me a three, two, one,

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:34.030
microscope vision.

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:34.863
Okay.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:39.623
It kind of looks like we're
underwater or something.

00:07:40.936 --> 00:07:41.769
What do you notice?

00:07:41.769 --> 00:07:43.047
There's nothing really going on yet.

00:07:43.047 --> 00:07:44.890
You can kind of adjust the image.

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:47.670
But like I said, I don't
have a sample on here.

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.150
You're looking at kind of the glass.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:54.600
Now I'm gonna just move my
hand across the microscope

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.330
and let me know what you see.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:06.603
That's my hand.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.893
Can you see my fingernail?

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:13.430
So we're pretty zoomed in.

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:15.690
That's how zoomed in we are right now.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:17.600
That's a familiar object.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:22.363
Now, we are going to put
on our mystery test object.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.580
So I'm gonna, just like before,
I'm gonna slide it across.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:29.333
Well, okay, I'll stop right there.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.470
Using your skills of
observation and your eyes mainly

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:35.593
in this case.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:39.070
Meat, that's an interesting
observation maybe

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:43.440
because it's got that sort of texture.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.440
Lots of little kind of on
some gritty worm, sponge,

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.310
I like that, cheeseburger.

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:53.190
'Cause it looks like it's
got that yellow cheese.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:54.760
I like it.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:56.930
Rock, I'll move around a little bit more.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:57.763
Keep them coming.

00:08:57.763 --> 00:08:58.690
Keep these guesses come in.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:00.373
I'm gonna move around a little more.

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:09.503
Ellie got it.

00:09:09.503 --> 00:09:10.336
Nice job.

00:09:10.336 --> 00:09:11.700
A pencil.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.850
This is a pencil.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:18.500
I think it's even the
classic if I move over here.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.610
Does anyone have a pencil
just like this near them?

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.787
This is like a Ticonderoga pencil.

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:27.700
Yeah, I see one.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:28.620
Awesome.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:33.310
So we are looking at a pencil

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:36.560
and I love we got some questions there.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:37.393
How?

00:09:37.393 --> 00:09:38.226
How do we do that?

00:09:38.226 --> 00:09:40.680
So I took this and I put it right on

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.510
the part of the microscope
where the action happens.

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:43.740
This is called the stage.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:46.130
This is also where I put my hand.

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:49.970
This image went up through
this kind of black thing.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:52.740
We'll get into what's
inside there in a minute.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:55.210
And then it went all the way up.

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:57.230
And in this case, it went up to a camera

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.680
and that camera, and you see this cord

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:02.510
goes all the way to my computer,

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:04.010
and it attaches to all of you.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.560
A lot of the time when I use a microscope

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.650
I don't have the camera and I use these.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:11.820
Then these allow me to look straight down

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:14.730
and see the same thing.

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:17.620
So what you just saw is
exactly what you would see

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:20.090
if you were here with me
and able to look through

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:22.443
these eyepieces is what we call them.

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:26.230
So an eyepiece if I take one
right here, I can show you,

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:27.960
whoa.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.610
What happens when I hold this up?

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:32.943
That's kind of bizarre.

00:10:38.429 --> 00:10:40.120
I just turned my head upside down.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:41.300
I know, it's kind of cool.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:43.810
So lenses can do all sorts
of interesting things

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:50.313
but basically they take an image
and they can manipulate it.

00:10:50.313 --> 00:10:52.660
They can make it bigger or smaller

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:54.163
or sometimes they can flip it.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:56.670
I'm wearing them on my face right now.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.680
Does anyone else have
lenses on their face?

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:01.063
Yes, I see some glasses.

00:11:01.902 --> 00:11:04.160
So a similar thing happens here.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.030
It's a smaller lens, but
it's basically the same thing

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:09.230
as what's inside of my
microscope right now.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.420
Are we ready for some living samples?

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:15.700
Does anyone have any
questions before I do this

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:20.700
about how my microscope
works or what we just saw?

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:25.966
GA.

00:11:25.966 --> 00:11:29.160
- [GA] When you put something
on the microscope head,

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.883
how do you see it from the lenses?

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:33.453
- That's great.

00:11:33.453 --> 00:11:34.286
That's a great question.

00:11:34.286 --> 00:11:37.750
So like I said before, up here is a camera

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:41.110
just like a camera that
you take pictures with.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:42.180
It's just like,

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:47.180
imagine if I'm pointing
that down through a hole

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:49.520
at the top of my microscope,

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:53.280
you can kind of see where this tube is.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:56.570
And that points all the way
down through the big lens,

00:11:56.570 --> 00:11:57.970
which is here.

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:00.190
So basically, my camera's
pointing right through

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:04.268
and then just like the camera
that I'm using on my computer

00:12:04.268 --> 00:12:07.000
it just goes straight into Zoom.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:10.500
So I have a cable and
it runs all the way in

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:13.960
and I can just select
it as one of the cameras

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:17.319
that you get to see my picture with.

00:12:17.319 --> 00:12:18.790
I'm not sure who was first

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:22.323
but I see William and then
Kate have their hands up.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:28.570
William, why don't you go ahead?

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.263
- [William] What's inside the microscope?

00:12:37.748 --> 00:12:38.880
- That's a great one.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:42.393
So if I take my microscope.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:46.653
Let me see if I can do this.

00:12:52.081 --> 00:12:55.920
I just took the body of the microscope off

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:57.280
and I'm even gonna flip it

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:03.230
so you can see those are
the two lenses that go down.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:06.620
One comes from each eye

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:08.450
and they go down

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:09.590
and then that's basically it.

00:13:09.590 --> 00:13:12.980
There's a couple of
lenses that are stacked

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:15.770
on top of each other inside of here.

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:20.770
And then the path of that
image will either go to my eyes

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:23.623
or up to the camera here.

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:26.940
And down here, you can
kind of guess what this is.

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:29.933
I'll put the body of my
microscope aside for a second,

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:32.313
hold this up.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:34.950
This is pretty straightforward.

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:37.193
What do you see going on here?

00:13:37.193 --> 00:13:38.893
I can actually turn it off and on.

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:45.193
This is the main thing that
this part of microscope does.

