00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:21.770
Liam, I see your hand up.

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:22.603
Go ahead.

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:32.033
- [Liam] I think I'd
like to see a Chrysalis.

00:30:36.174 --> 00:30:37.532
- I love that idea.

00:30:37.532 --> 00:30:39.843
Yeah. So could we look at something

00:30:41.190 --> 00:30:43.590
that was actually going through

00:30:43.590 --> 00:30:48.180
some sort of a metamorphosis,
something like that?

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:49.250
I heard you all.

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:52.510
Might've been learning about a life cycle

00:30:52.510 --> 00:30:53.760
or life cycles this year.

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:54.693
Is that right?

00:30:56.190 --> 00:30:58.168
I do have a sample.

00:30:58.168 --> 00:31:03.168
I don't have a Chrysalis,
but I do have a few samples

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:09.070
of another animal that are
going through a life cycle.

00:31:09.070 --> 00:31:12.010
Should we have that be
our last sample for today

00:31:12.010 --> 00:31:12.843
that we look at?

00:31:12.843 --> 00:31:13.820
You guys can try and you can guess,

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:16.611
you can try to guess
what it's gonna become.

00:31:16.611 --> 00:31:17.923
Sian, go ahead.

00:31:23.674 --> 00:31:26.360
- [Sian] I forgot what I was gonna say.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:27.504
- That's okay.

00:31:27.504 --> 00:31:29.613
And I did actually mean,

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:34.780
I was curious what other animals
people wanted to look at.

00:31:34.780 --> 00:31:36.520
And I see some have come up in the chat.

00:31:36.520 --> 00:31:38.834
Someone said maybe a bear or a wolf.

00:31:38.834 --> 00:31:40.557
That would be cool to
look under a microscope

00:31:40.557 --> 00:31:42.860
but you have to choose a part of its body.

00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:47.860
Maybe just it's fur, or you
could put its teeth, a tooth

00:31:48.512 --> 00:31:51.498
under the microscope and
see what that looks like.

00:31:51.498 --> 00:31:53.983
Butterflies would be really beautiful.

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:01.210
To look at my lifecycle sample,

00:32:01.210 --> 00:32:04.840
I've got four different stages of its life

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:05.870
and they're preserved.

00:32:05.870 --> 00:32:10.870
So what that means is it's
in a special kind of plastic.

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:13.930
So it's kind of like frozen in time.

00:32:13.930 --> 00:32:15.880
And we're gonna start off with this one.

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:17.743
They're really tiny, little specs.

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:23.646
So I'll put on our microscope
vision one last time.

00:32:23.646 --> 00:32:25.080
So let's do a

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:28.793
three, two, one.

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:31.910
Beautiful.

00:32:31.910 --> 00:32:36.910
And so, this is the beginning
of our mystery animal's life.

00:32:37.880 --> 00:32:41.083
Starts out like this.

00:32:44.500 --> 00:32:46.860
A lot of animals start out
as something like this.

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:48.660
What does this look like to you all?

00:32:51.725 --> 00:32:53.860
Well, those are guesses
about what it could be

00:32:53.860 --> 00:32:55.240
but exactly, Claire got it.

00:32:55.240 --> 00:32:56.400
These are its eggs.

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:58.150
We don't know what kind
of eggs they are yet

00:32:58.150 --> 00:33:01.933
but these are certainly
the eggs of this animal.

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:05.460
They're kind of just small and round.

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:07.590
If I turn them over to the other side

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:10.323
maybe they'll look different on this side.

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:17.750
No, just kind of round,
not extremely interesting.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:19.320
So I'll take this one off

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:22.210
and we'll go to the
second stage of its life.

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:23.690
Here we go.

00:33:23.690 --> 00:33:24.860
Put this one under a microscope.

00:33:24.860 --> 00:33:26.173
It's a little bigger now.

00:33:31.001 --> 00:33:31.834
And it's got these.

00:33:31.834 --> 00:33:34.948
Wow, it's got these pretty giant eyes.

00:33:34.948 --> 00:33:36.750
We've got a guess for this one.

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:38.450
Now, maybe a beetle or a cockroach.

00:33:38.450 --> 00:33:42.930
We've moved away from our
idea that this is a frog.

00:33:42.930 --> 00:33:47.540
It's got longer, pointier legs.

00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:50.483
Little hooks on them.

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:56.653
Pretty wild shape.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.570
If I look in at the very front of it,

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:03.263
I can see it's even got
these almost like pinchers.

00:34:05.770 --> 00:34:06.733
That's pretty wild.

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:11.323
Then the plot thickens, it
gets a little bit bigger.

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:13.963
Wow.

00:34:15.297 --> 00:34:16.360
Now, I'm gonna zoom out

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:18.920
so we can try to see it a little better

00:34:18.920 --> 00:34:23.920
but it got a little bigger, but
has all the same body parts.

00:34:23.980 --> 00:34:25.790
So this is kind of like how we grow.

00:34:25.790 --> 00:34:28.230
This body didn't really
change shape it just,

00:34:28.230 --> 00:34:31.610
everything got a bit bigger.

00:34:31.610 --> 00:34:35.820
So now the whole screen right
now is just this eyeball.

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:37.620
Let me zoom in on that a little bit.

00:34:49.220 --> 00:34:50.053
A termite.

00:34:50.053 --> 00:34:51.520
That's like a guess.

00:34:51.520 --> 00:34:54.400
Now this is where it gets pretty bizarre.

00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:57.133
So this animal has a life cycle where,

00:34:58.610 --> 00:35:02.683
we've been observing and it
looks like it's got six legs.

00:35:03.610 --> 00:35:07.420
It's got these kinds of pointy
structures all around it

00:35:11.770 --> 00:35:13.610
but now it's gonna change.

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:15.583
So this is it as an adult.

00:35:26.571 --> 00:35:30.270
It still has its legs and its eyes.

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:32.130
So I recognize the center of his body,

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:36.220
but out from the sides,
nice work, everybody,

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:37.053
you got it.

00:35:37.053 --> 00:35:37.886
Dragonfly.

00:35:37.886 --> 00:35:39.120
So it is very similar to an ant

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:42.820
because a lot of ants kind
of grow wings like this

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:43.653
as they get bigger.

00:35:43.653 --> 00:35:46.640
But you'll notice the wings
of this are just gigantic.

00:35:46.640 --> 00:35:49.650
They go off into all,

00:35:49.650 --> 00:35:53.873
they get wings that are
a couple inches long.

00:35:54.790 --> 00:35:57.310
Sometimes you get dragonflies

00:35:57.310 --> 00:35:59.410
that are the size of your head.

00:35:59.410 --> 00:36:02.800
Really big, really, really big.

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:03.923
This one is dead.

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:10.560
But it's a kind of species
we might find around here

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:12.210
if we wanted to.

00:36:12.210 --> 00:36:13.080
I'll show you what they look like

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:14.430
without the microscope now.

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:23.223
So we started off here,

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:29.330
and then we went through these
different stages of its life.

00:36:30.100 --> 00:36:33.173
And then this was the full-size dragonfly.

00:36:35.170 --> 00:36:36.620
I think is kind of beautiful.

00:36:42.040 --> 00:36:44.310
And I'll keep in mind
some of the other samples

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:45.500
you all suggested.

00:36:45.500 --> 00:36:50.500
I too would be very interested
to see what the hair

00:36:51.540 --> 00:36:55.970
or the teeth of some larger
predators might look like.

00:36:55.970 --> 00:36:58.276
So I'll have to keep an eye out for them.

00:36:58.276 --> 00:37:03.276
And sometimes when we
travel to new places,

00:37:05.187 --> 00:37:10.187
you can find samples or
things that you wanna explore

00:37:10.920 --> 00:37:11.753
and discover.

00:37:11.753 --> 00:37:12.940
Like I said at the very beginning,

00:37:12.940 --> 00:37:16.032
you can already do this with
the tools that you have,

00:37:16.032 --> 00:37:19.890
your eyes, and your ears, and your nose,

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:23.740
making observations about living things.

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:26.410
And hopefully pretty soon,

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:30.507
we'll be able to bring a
laboratory like the BioBus

00:37:30.507 --> 00:37:35.150
so you can use all this
equipment with your own hands

00:37:35.150 --> 00:37:38.063
and get to try it out for yourself.

00:37:39.260 --> 00:37:41.247
Does anyone have any last question

00:37:41.247 --> 00:37:43.823
before we wrap up for today?

00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:53.120
I love the emoji cat,
not even the symbols.

00:37:55.350 --> 00:38:00.350
I'm surprised my cat often
likes to make little appearances

00:38:00.810 --> 00:38:02.640
while I'm teaching.

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:03.940
I think he's taking a nap.

00:38:06.270 --> 00:38:08.460
- [Jeanne] I think Ava,
you are saying in the chat

00:38:08.460 --> 00:38:09.390
that you have a question.

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:12.720
Feel free to unmute yourself
and ask your question please.

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:13.553
- Your hand was up.

00:38:13.553 --> 00:38:14.690
I didn't see it.

00:38:17.307 --> 00:38:20.023
- [Ava] How often do you use microscopes?

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:23.210
- That's a great question.

00:38:23.210 --> 00:38:27.137
I use them pretty much every day.

00:38:29.790 --> 00:38:32.540
Different scientists use them
all different amounts of.

00:38:33.700 --> 00:38:38.700
I used to work in a different
lab and it was near the sea.

00:38:39.100 --> 00:38:41.204
I studied things that live in the ocean

00:38:41.204 --> 00:38:45.780
and I would spend like an entire month

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:47.800
where I'd just be out collecting samples.

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:49.390
So wouldn't use a microscope at all.

00:38:49.390 --> 00:38:52.280
And then I'd spend three
months just in the lab

00:38:52.280 --> 00:38:53.730
looking at them under the microscope.

00:38:53.730 --> 00:38:55.570
And then I wouldn't go back
to the microscope again

00:38:55.570 --> 00:38:56.403
for awhile.

00:38:56.403 --> 00:38:58.930
I'd be writing what I found.

00:38:58.930 --> 00:39:02.200
So there's kind of phases of science

00:39:02.200 --> 00:39:04.050
and some of them include the microscope

00:39:04.050 --> 00:39:05.250
and some of them do not.

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:09.780
So McLean, I also saw Paige you had

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:11.840
your real hand raised.

00:39:11.840 --> 00:39:14.280
So I wanna make sure I
hear from some voices

00:39:14.280 --> 00:39:16.128
I haven't heard from yet.

00:39:16.128 --> 00:39:18.653
So maybe let's let Paige go
ahead and ask a question.

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:22.540
And then I see Ellie,
you've got your hand up too.

00:39:24.912 --> 00:39:27.833
- [Paige] Can you take
pictures on microscopes?

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:30.910
- Yeah. So this same camera.

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:32.210
That's a great question.

00:39:32.210 --> 00:39:35.600
It can take pictures.

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:38.610
And that is a really important way

00:39:38.610 --> 00:39:42.230
that scientists communicate
with one another now.

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:44.900
They take pictures and even videos

00:39:44.900 --> 00:39:47.040
of what they see under the microscope

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:49.792
and then they share that
with other scientists

00:39:49.792 --> 00:39:52.770
so that they are able to see

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:54.720
the discoveries that other people made.

00:39:56.310 --> 00:39:58.660
And sometimes you can
make art with it too.

00:39:58.660 --> 00:40:03.660
So that's one of my favorite
places where we can cross over

00:40:04.260 --> 00:40:06.183
between science and art.

00:40:09.160 --> 00:40:10.630
Ellie, I saw your hand was up.

00:40:10.630 --> 00:40:14.580
And then Claire,

00:40:14.580 --> 00:40:17.773
and then I think we have time
for one very last question.

00:40:22.300 --> 00:40:27.300
- [Ellie] Have you ever seen
a bigger animal up close?

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:32.873
- Yeah, definitely.

00:40:34.910 --> 00:40:38.160
We used to work in a
laboratory with BioBus

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:40.870
that had a big corn snake.

00:40:40.870 --> 00:40:42.960
So that was when we used to
put it under the microscope

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:44.563
a lot and a turtle.

00:40:45.410 --> 00:40:48.920
So this microscope, we can
look at pretty large animals

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:50.490
and I have another one that's handheld.

00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:53.580
So we can even look at
our hair, and our clothes,

00:40:53.580 --> 00:40:54.900
and our eyes.

00:40:54.900 --> 00:40:57.453
We're a pretty big animal
I would say, right?

00:40:59.310 --> 00:41:00.143
Okay.

00:41:00.143 --> 00:41:01.100
Claire and then McLean

00:41:01.100 --> 00:41:03.973
and then we're gonna have to wrap up.

00:41:05.710 --> 00:41:07.087
- [Claire] Do microscopes have lights

00:41:07.087 --> 00:41:10.440
so you can see it when
there's light on it?

00:41:10.440 --> 00:41:11.660
- Exactly. Yep.

00:41:11.660 --> 00:41:14.470
So remember that bottom
part of the microscope,

00:41:14.470 --> 00:41:16.020
it has two different lights

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:18.770
and that's the only thing that
those lights are there for,

00:41:18.770 --> 00:41:23.770
is so that the light can
travel through our lenses.

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:29.740
Without a light then it would be so dark

00:41:29.740 --> 00:41:31.331
that you couldn't see anything.

00:41:31.331 --> 00:41:33.867
But different microscopes,

00:41:33.867 --> 00:41:38.190
they can use lasers or fluorescent light

00:41:38.190 --> 00:41:42.070
or even electrons like
electricity to make an image.

00:41:42.070 --> 00:41:43.570
They don't need to have light.

00:41:44.413 --> 00:41:49.413
Okay. I think McLean, and
then we're gonna wrap up.

00:41:52.180 --> 00:41:53.013
Go ahead.

00:42:04.230 --> 00:42:05.703
You might be frozen for me.

00:42:09.290 --> 00:42:14.290
Okay. If you want, you can
add questions in the chat.

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:15.817
And I see there's another one here.

00:42:15.817 --> 00:42:18.370
"How do microscopes make things look big?"

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:23.370
So inside of it, the thing that
is really doing all the work

00:42:23.800 --> 00:42:26.760
is extremely simple.

00:42:26.760 --> 00:42:30.220
It's just a lens that's made out of glass.

00:42:30.220 --> 00:42:31.700
And I have one here,

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:35.510
so you can see it's just a
piece of glass and it's curved.

00:42:35.510 --> 00:42:37.943
If I hold it in front of my face.

00:42:41.060 --> 00:42:43.263
So this is kind of like a
microscope and that's all it is.

00:42:43.263 --> 00:42:45.280
It's a little piece of curved glass

00:42:46.750 --> 00:42:49.053
and I can make things look really big.

00:42:50.970 --> 00:42:52.390
That's pretty cool, right?

00:42:52.390 --> 00:42:54.190
Lenses can be made out of
lots of different things

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:56.270
but most of the ones that
we use in microscopes

00:42:56.270 --> 00:42:57.370
are made out of glass.

00:43:02.090 --> 00:43:03.973
Well, this was wonderful.

00:43:08.389 --> 00:43:11.556
So the question that I didn't get was,

00:43:12.820 --> 00:43:15.320
have I ever gone on a
Safari to look at animals?

00:43:16.410 --> 00:43:18.010
I haven't been on a Safari

00:43:18.010 --> 00:43:21.430
but I have been on field.

00:43:21.430 --> 00:43:23.970
I've done fieldwork around the world.

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:28.650
So the coolest place that
I got to work for my work

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:29.970
was in Ireland.

00:43:29.970 --> 00:43:34.650
I worked in the world's largest tide pool.

00:43:34.650 --> 00:43:36.630
It was like the size of a lake.

00:43:36.630 --> 00:43:39.330
And it was full of all sorts
of interesting sea creatures.

00:43:39.330 --> 00:43:44.220
So that was my version
of going on to a Safari

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:47.070
because I study things
that live in the sea.

00:43:47.070 --> 00:43:48.466
- [Jeanne] That sounds amazing.

00:43:48.466 --> 00:43:50.593
- Yeah. It's pretty great.

00:43:51.558 --> 00:43:53.710
If you have any more questions,

00:43:53.710 --> 00:43:56.890
you can always tell your teacher

00:43:56.890 --> 00:43:58.883
and she can send them to me.

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:03.793
And hopefully, we'll get
to meet in-person someday.

00:44:05.620 --> 00:44:06.950
- Thanks so much, Mollie.

00:44:06.950 --> 00:44:10.660
We're so excited to have
the BioBus with us today

00:44:10.660 --> 00:44:14.350
and we're so excited to have
all of you with us today.

00:44:14.350 --> 00:44:17.913
Thank you for bringing
such wonderful questions.

00:44:18.949 --> 00:44:23.440
Having Lab Backstage allows
us to get science anywhere

00:44:23.440 --> 00:44:24.570
it needs to be.

00:44:24.570 --> 00:44:28.860
So thanks to the internet
and thanks to Zoom for that,

00:44:28.860 --> 00:44:29.693
I guess.

00:44:31.210 --> 00:44:35.920
Anyway, friends, let's
give scientist Mollie

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:37.780
a big round of applause.

00:44:37.780 --> 00:44:39.180
This is how I like to do it.

00:44:41.850 --> 00:44:43.060
All right everybody,

00:44:43.060 --> 00:44:44.290
have a great day

00:44:44.290 --> 00:44:47.020
and happy sciencing.

00:44:47.020 --> 00:44:48.430
- Thank you so much.

00:44:48.430 --> 00:44:49.830
Fabulous.

00:44:49.830 --> 00:44:50.663
- Thank you.

00:44:51.626 --> 00:44:52.626
All right, take care

